  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0045_d78f39a45c9f.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 247px;\" /></p><p>Wavelength of photons released during de-excitation is given by,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>&#955;</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mstyle></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></mstyle></mfrac></mrow></mfenced></math>.</p><p>Clearly, shortest wavelength will correspond to highest energy difference in de-excitation, which in the given figure will correspond to transition from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></math> to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
